% Learning the parameters of a program from data.
% From the dPASP tutorial:
% https://kamel-usp.github.io/pages/learn_dpasp.html#learning-the-parameters-of-programs

% Crime rate. The `?::` marks this probability as *learnable*: it starts
% unknown and is fitted to the data by the #learn directive below.
?::burglary.
% Daily earthquake probabilities.
0.05::earthquake(heavy); 0.15::earthquake(mild); 0.8::earthquake(none).
% Error rates.
0.90::alarm :- burglary, earthquake(heavy).
0.85::alarm :- burglary, earthquake(mild).
0.80::alarm :- burglary, earthquake(none).
0.05::alarm :- not burglary, earthquake(mild).
0.10::alarm :- not burglary, earthquake(heavy).
% Help of neighbors.
0.8::calls(X) :- alarm, neighbor(X).
0.1::calls(X) :- not alarm, neighbor(X).
% Bert and Ernie are Elmo's neighbors.
neighbor(bert). neighbor(ernie).
% What is the probability of a burglary at Elmo's?
#query burglary.

% We learn from a CSV containing Bert and Ernie's calls.
%
% Prefer your own uploaded copy of the file over this URL. Use the upload
% button in the file browser on the left, then replace the URL below with just
% the file name:
%
%     #learn "elmo.csv", niters = 10.
%
% Programs run with the uploaded-files folder as their working directory, so a
% bare name is all that is needed. Three reasons to do it:
%
%   * The CSV is read while the program is being *parsed*, so an unreachable
%     URL fails the whole run before any inference happens.
%   * The runner container is meant to be locked down — once it has no
%     outbound network, only uploaded files will work.
%   * Your own data is the point. Swap in a CSV of your own with one column
%     per observed atom (here `calls(bert)` and `calls(ernie)`) and one row
%     per observation, holding 0 or 1.
#learn "https://www.ime.usp.br/~renatolg/dpasp/elmo.csv", niters = 10.

% Select the max-ent semantics.
#semantics maxent.
